IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pic-dates`

-- Personal Injury Commission Act 2020 (NSW) -- the provisions a claimant meets, from the seat of the claimant: the
-- injured worker, the person injured in a motor accident, or the police officer under the police officer support
-- scheme, who is a party to proceedings before the Personal Injury Commission.
--
-- SEAT: the claimant. Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Who may represent me?", "May the insurer
-- have a lawyer when I have none?", "Do I commit an offence if I do not comply?", "Can what I said at the Commission be
-- used against me in court?", "Can my proceedings be dismissed?", "Can I appeal?", "Can the error be corrected?", "How do
-- I enforce the amount I was awarded?", "Can I take my matter to the District Court instead?", "Can I complain about my
-- insurer, and by when must the complaint be dealt with?". The Commission, its President, registrars, assessors and the
-- Independent Review Officer get no seat: what they do is encoded as what it means for the claimant.
--
-- Provisions: s 5 (definitions, as used); ss 25-28 (federal jurisdiction: the District Court instead of the Commission);
-- s 31(5), (6) (proceedings outside the State); ss 38, 40 (assessors, reviewers and mediators not compellable); s 42(3)
-- (the duty to co-operate, as a comment); s 48 (representation); s 49 (directions to produce or provide, with a
-- regulative rule); s 51 (summonses); s 52(3) (decision without a hearing); s 53 (replacing a member); s 54 (dismissal);
-- s 56 (finality); s 57 (reconsideration and obvious errors); s 58 (publication); s 59 (recovery of amounts ordered);
-- s 64(3), (4) (officers' protection); Sch 3 cll 6-8 and Sch 3A cll 5-6 (conciliation; statements not admissible; evidence
-- on oath); Sch 4 cl 7 (assessment-conference statements); Sch 5 cll 1, 7(3), 8, 9 (complaints to the Independent
-- Review Officer, with a regulative rule; ILARS). Out: see NOTES.md.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no (a "means" definition, a
-- condition introduced by "if", "only if", "unless" or "provided", a flat "not" or "cannot"), or NOTHING where the Act
-- does not answer on the facts given; each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind" comment. Judgments the Act leaves to the
-- Commission, the President, the District Court or the Independent Review Officer are asked as inputs, never decided.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; version as at 27 September 2024; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not
-- for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Personal Injury Commission Act 2020`

§§ `The nouns`

-- s 5(1) "Commission Division means (a) the Workers Compensation Division, or (b) the Motor Accidents Division, or (c) the
-- Police Officer Support Scheme Division."
DECLARE `Commission Division` IS ONE OF
    `the Workers Compensation Division`
    `the Motor Accidents Division`
    `the Police Officer Support Scheme Division`

-- s 48(1) "a lawyer or an agent"; s 48(8) "agent, in relation to proceedings allocated to the Workers Compensation
-- Division, means (a) an officer of an industrial organisation of employers or employees registered under the Industrial
-- Relations Act 1996, or (b) an officer of an association of employers or employees registered under the Fair Work Act
-- 2009 of the Commonwealth, or (c) a person employed by a licensed insurer or former licensed insurer or by a
-- self-insurer ..., or (d) a person employed by a law practice ...".
DECLARE `My representative` IS ONE OF
    `an Australian legal practitioner`
    `an officer of an organisation of employers or employees registered under the Industrial Relations Act 1996`
    `an officer of an association of employers or employees registered under the Fair Work Act 2009`
    `a person employed by a licensed insurer or former licensed insurer or by a self-insurer`
    `a person employed by a law practice`
    `someone else`

-- s 48(4) the only grounds on which leave may be granted to an insurer.
DECLARE `Ground for the insurer's leave` IS ONE OF
    `the representation would let the matter be dealt with more efficiently given its complexity`
    `it would be unfair because the insurer cannot represent itself effectively`
    `it would be unfair given fairness between the insurer and the other parties`
    `no ground the Commission is satisfied of`

-- The requirements whose breach is an offence (ss 49(3), 51(3); Sch 3 cl 8(2); Sch 3A cl 6(2)).
DECLARE `Requirement made of me` IS ONE OF
    `a direction to produce documents or provide information or give a consent`
    `a summons to attend a conference or hearing`
    `a requirement to give evidence on oath or answer a question in the Workers Compensation Division`
    `a requirement to give evidence on oath or answer a question in the Police Officer Support Scheme Division`

-- The court proceedings in which a statement made at the Commission might be offered in evidence (Sch 3 cl 7;
-- Sch 4 cl 7).
DECLARE `The court proceedings` IS ONE OF
    `court proceedings on a claim for work injury damages`
    `court proceedings under section 235A or 235C of the 1998 Act or for fraud under the Crimes Act 1900 on such a claim`
    `court proceedings on a motor accident claim`
    `court proceedings under Division 6.6 of the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017 or Part 4.6 of the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999`
    `other court proceedings`

-- ss 32, 38, 40, 64(3), s 5(1) "relevant Commission officer".
DECLARE `Commission officer` IS ONE OF
    `a registrar`
    `a medical assessor`
    `a merit reviewer`
    `a mediator`
    `a member of the Commission`
    `someone else working for the Commission`

-- s 53(1) the reasons a member may be replaced.
DECLARE `Reason for replacing the member` IS ONE OF
    `the member has become unavailable`
    `the member has ceased to be a member`
    `the member has ceased to have a qualification the proceedings require`
    `some other reason`

-- s 57(6) examples of obvious errors.
DECLARE `Kind of error` IS ONE OF
    `a clerical or typographical error`
    `an accidental slip or omission`
    `a defect of form`
    `an inconsistency between the stated decision and the stated reasons`
    `some other error`

-- s 58(1) the decisions whose details must be published.
DECLARE `Kind of decision` IS ONE OF
    `a decision of the Commission`
    `a decision of a merit reviewer under Division 7.4 of the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017`
    `a decision of a review panel for merit reviewers under that Division 7.4`
    `a decision of a medical Appeal Panel under Part 7 of Chapter 7 of the 1998 Act`
    `a decision of a medical review panel under Part 3.4 of the 1999 Act or Division 7.5 of the 2017 Act`
    `some other decision`

-- s 25 "compensation claim means (a) a claim for damages to which the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 applies, or
-- (b) a claim for statutory benefits to which the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017 applies, or (c) a claim for damages to
-- which the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017 applies, or (c1) a claim under the police officer support scheme, or (d) a
-- claim for compensation or work injury damages to which the Workplace Injury Management and Workers Compensation Act
-- 1998 applies."
DECLARE `My claim` IS ONE OF
    `a claim for damages under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999`
    `a claim for statutory benefits under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017`
    `a claim for damages under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017`
    `a claim under the police officer support scheme`
    `a claim for workers compensation or work injury damages under the 1998 Act`
    `some other claim`

-- The regulative rules' parties and acts.
DECLARE `Commission party` IS ONE OF
    `the person directed`
    `the Independent Review Officer`

DECLARE `Commission act` IS ONE OF
    `comply with the direction`
    `deal with the complaint`

-- What a direction under s 49 means for me.
DECLARE `What the direction means for me` IS ONE OF
    `I complied with the direction in time`
    `I did not comply in time but had a reasonable excuse, so section 49 makes it no offence and does not keep the material out`
    `I did not comply in time without reasonable excuse, so it is an offence and I cannot have the material admitted`
    `the time for complying has not yet run out`

-- Where my complaint to the Independent Review Officer stands under Sch 5 cl 8(4).
DECLARE `Where my complaint stands` IS ONE OF
    `the Officer dealt with my complaint in time`
    `the Officer did not deal with my complaint in time`
    `the time for dealing with my complaint has not yet run out`

§§ `s 31(5) -- proceedings outside the State`

-- s 31(5) "The President may direct that proceedings be conducted outside the State (a) if requested by a party to the
-- proceedings, or (b) with the consent of the parties to the proceedings." s 31(6): the President must weigh the
-- parties' interests and wishes, the nature and complexity of the proceedings, timeliness and cost, and other matters
-- (a judgment, not asked). "the parties" is read as all of them (fork F1).
@export Can the President direct that my proceedings be conducted outside New South Wales (s 31(5))?
GIVEN `a party has asked for it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, or any other party to the proceedings, has asked for the proceedings to be conducted outside New South Wales
      `all the parties consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties to the proceedings consent to their being conducted outside New South Wales
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my proceedings be conducted outside New South Wales` MEANS
    IF `a party has asked for it` OR `all the parties consent`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 48 -- representation`

-- s 48(1) "A person who is a party to proceedings before the Commission is entitled to be represented by an Australian
-- legal practitioner or by an agent." (2) "The Commission may refuse to permit a party to be represented by an agent if
-- of the opinion that the agent does not have sufficient authority to make binding decisions on behalf of the party."
-- s 48(8) defines "agent" only for the Workers Compensation Division (a "means" list); elsewhere "agent" is undefined.
@export Am I entitled to be represented before the Commission by this person (s 48(1), (2), (8))?
GIVEN `the Division` IS A `Commission Division` @desc The Division of the Commission your proceedings are in. One of: "the Workers Compensation Division", "the Motor Accidents Division", "the Police Officer Support Scheme Division"
      `the representative` IS A `My representative` @desc Who would represent you. One of: "an Australian legal practitioner", "an officer of an organisation of employers or employees registered under the Industrial Relations Act 1996", "an officer of an association of employers or employees registered under the Fair Work Act 2009", "a person employed by a licensed insurer or former licensed insurer or by a self-insurer", "a person employed by a law practice", "someone else"
      `the Commission refused the agent for lack of authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission has refused to let this person (if not a lawyer) represent you because it is of the opinion that the person does not have sufficient authority to make binding decisions for you
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I entitled to be represented by this person` MEANS
    IF `the representative` EQUALS `an Australian legal practitioner`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF `the Commission refused the agent for lack of authority`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `the Division` EQUALS `the Workers Compensation Division`
              THEN IF `the representative` EQUALS `someone else`
                   THEN JUST FALSE
                   ELSE JUST TRUE
              ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind ("agent" is undefined outside the Workers Compensation Division)

-- s 48(3) "In proceedings in respect of a claim within the meaning of the Workplace Injury Management and Workers
-- Compensation Act 1998, the Commission must refuse to permit an insurer to be represented by an Australian legal
-- practitioner if the claimant is not represented by an Australian legal practitioner unless leave is granted by the
-- Commission under subsection (4)." (4) "... may ... grant leave ... only if satisfied that (a) ... more efficiently ...,
-- or (b) ... unable to represent the insurer effectively, or (c) ... fairness between the insurer and other parties".
-- (5) "The Commission may at any time revoke leave it has granted". Otherwise s 48(1) entitles the insurer, as a party,
-- to a lawyer.
@export If I have no lawyer in my workers compensation proceedings, may the insurer be represented by a lawyer (s 48(1), (3)-(5))?
GIVEN `the proceedings are about a claim under the 1998 Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are about a claim within the meaning of the Workplace Injury Management and Workers Compensation Act 1998 (a workers compensation claim)
      `I am represented by a lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are represented by an Australian legal practitioner
      `the Commission granted the insurer leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission has granted the insurer leave to be represented by a lawyer
      `the ground the Commission was satisfied of` IS A `Ground for the insurer's leave` @desc The ground the Commission was satisfied of in granting leave. One of: "the representation would let the matter be dealt with more efficiently given its complexity", "it would be unfair because the insurer cannot represent itself effectively", "it would be unfair given fairness between the insurer and the other parties", "no ground the Commission is satisfied of"
      `the Commission revoked the leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission has since revoked that leave
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May the insurer be represented by a lawyer` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the proceedings are about a claim under the 1998 Act`) OR `I am represented by a lawyer`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF     `the Commission granted the insurer leave`
            AND (NOT (`the ground the Commission was satisfied of` EQUALS `no ground the Commission is satisfied of`))
            AND (NOT `the Commission revoked the leave`)
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 48(6) "A party ... is entitled to representation or assistance (for example, the assistance of an interpreter) as may
-- be necessary to enable the party to communicate adequately at any conference or hearing."
@export Am I entitled to an interpreter or other assistance at a conference or hearing (s 48(6))?
GIVEN `I need it to communicate adequately` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you need the interpreter, or other representation or assistance, to communicate adequately at the conference or hearing
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I entitled to an interpreter or other assistance` MEANS
    IF `I need it to communicate adequately`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited by its own wording, "as may be necessary"; the case falls outside the limit)

-- s 48(7) "The Commission must take into account any written submission prepared by an Australian legal practitioner
-- acting for a party ... and submitted by or on behalf of the party (whether or not the party is represented by an
-- Australian legal practitioner at any conference or hearing ...)."
@export Must the Commission take into account a written submission my lawyer prepared (s 48(7))?
GIVEN `a lawyer acting for me prepared it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the written submission was prepared by an Australian legal practitioner acting for you
      `it was submitted by me or for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was submitted to the Commission by you or on your behalf
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the Commission take my lawyer's written submission into account` MEANS
    IF `a lawyer acting for me prepared it` AND `it was submitted by me or for me`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent on other submissions)

§§ `ss 49, 51, Sch 3 cl 8, Sch 3A cl 6 -- directions, summonses and evidence`

-- s 42(3) every party, and every lawyer or other representative, "is under a duty to co-operate with the Commission ...
-- to participate in the processes of the Commission and to comply with directions and orders of the Commission". No
-- consequence is attached; it is not asked.
--
-- s 49(1) the Commission may direct any person "(a) to produce, at a time and place specified in the direction, specified
-- documents ..., or (b) to provide specified information within a time specified ..., or (c) to give within a time
-- specified ... any specified consent, authority or direction". (3) "A person must not, without reasonable excuse, fail
-- to comply with a direction given to the person. Maximum penalty--50 penalty units." Time 0 is the day the direction
-- was given, in days; the deadline is the last day it allows.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Commission party` `Commission act`
`s 49(1), (3) -- the person directed must comply within the time specified` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the person directed`
    MUST `comply with the direction`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the person directed` BECAUSE "the direction to produce documents, provide information or give a consent was not complied with within the time specified (s 49(1), (3))"

-- s 49(3) the offence (reasonable excuse a judgment, asked); s 49(4) "If a person does not, without reasonable excuse,
-- produce a document or provide information in compliance with a direction ..., the person cannot as a party to
-- proceedings before the Commission or a court have the document or information admitted in the proceedings."
@export I was given a direction by the Commission to produce documents, provide information or give a consent: did I comply in time, and if not, what follows (s 49(1), (3), (4))?
GIVEN `the day the direction was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commission gave you the direction
      `the last day the direction allows` IS A DATE @desc The day by which (or on which) the direction requires you to comply
      `I have complied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have produced the documents, provided the information or given the consent the direction specifies
      `the day I complied` IS A DATE @desc The day you complied (any day if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a reasonable excuse for not complying in time (decided by the Commission or a court; give its view)
GIVETH A `What the direction means for me`
`Did I comply with the direction in time` MEANS
    IF `I have complied`
    THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 49(1), (3) -- the person directed must comply within the time specified` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the person directed` `comply with the direction` (`the days from` `the day the direction was given` `to` `the day I complied`)))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `I complied with the direction in time`
         OTHERWISE `the consequence of not complying`
    ELSE IF `today` `is on or before` `the last day the direction allows`
         THEN `the time for complying has not yet run out`
         ELSE `the consequence of not complying`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `the day the direction was given` `to` `the last day the direction allows`
        `the consequence of not complying` MEANS
            IF `I had a reasonable excuse`
            THEN `I did not comply in time but had a reasonable excuse, so section 49 makes it no offence and does not keep the material out`
            ELSE `I did not comply in time without reasonable excuse, so it is an offence and I cannot have the material admitted`

@export If I did not produce a document or provide information as the Commission directed, can I still have it admitted in the proceedings before the Commission or a court (s 49(4))?
GIVEN `I failed to comply with the direction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you did not produce the document or provide the information in compliance with a direction the Commission gave you
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a reasonable excuse for that (decided by the Commission or the court; give its view)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I have the document or information admitted` MEANS
    IF `I failed to comply with the direction` AND (NOT `I had a reasonable excuse`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 49(4) does not apply; admission is for the rules of evidence or, before the Commission, s 43(2))

-- ss 49(3), 51(3), Sch 3 cl 8(2), Sch 3A cl 6(2): "A person must not, without reasonable excuse, fail to comply ...
-- Maximum penalty--50 penalty units." s 51(2) "If the proceedings are allocated to the Motor Accidents Division, a
-- summons may be issued only to a party to the proceedings." Sch 3 cl 8(3), Sch 3A cl 6(3) "A person is not required to
-- answer a question under this clause if the answer to that question would tend to incriminate the person of an
-- offence." A summons in the Motor Accidents Division to a non-party is read as not "a summons served ... under this
-- section" (fork F2).
@export If I do not comply with this requirement of the Commission, do I commit an offence (ss 49(3), 51(2), (3); Sch 3 cl 8; Sch 3A cl 6)?
GIVEN `the requirement` IS A `Requirement made of me` @desc What the Commission required of you. One of: "a direction to produce documents or provide information or give a consent", "a summons to attend a conference or hearing", "a requirement to give evidence on oath or answer a question in the Workers Compensation Division", "a requirement to give evidence on oath or answer a question in the Police Officer Support Scheme Division"
      `the Division` IS A `Commission Division` @desc The Division of the Commission the proceedings are in. One of: "the Workers Compensation Division", "the Motor Accidents Division", "the Police Officer Support Scheme Division"
      `I am a party to the proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a party to the proceedings
      `the answer would tend to incriminate me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the requirement is to answer a question whose answer would tend to incriminate you of an offence
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a reasonable excuse for not complying (decided by a court; give its view)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do I commit an offence by not complying` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `I had a reasonable excuse` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF     `the requirement` EQUALS `a summons to attend a conference or hearing`
              AND `the Division` EQUALS `the Motor Accidents Division`
              AND (NOT `I am a party to the proceedings`)
           THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `an evidence requirement` AND `the answer would tend to incriminate me` THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
    WHERE
        `an evidence requirement` MEANS
                `the requirement` EQUALS `a requirement to give evidence on oath or answer a question in the Workers Compensation Division`
            OR  `the requirement` EQUALS `a requirement to give evidence on oath or answer a question in the Police Officer Support Scheme Division`

-- The maximum penalty is 50 penalty units for each of the four requirements. A penalty unit's dollar value is set by
-- s 17 of the Crimes (Sentencing Procedure) Act 1999, not deposited; the answer is in penalty units. s 60: proceedings
-- for an offence are dealt with summarily in the Local Court and may be brought only by the Minister or with the written
-- consent of the Minister or a person the Minister authorises.
@export What is the most I can be fined, in penalty units, for not complying with this requirement without reasonable excuse (ss 49(3), 51(3); Sch 3 cl 8(2); Sch 3A cl 6(2))?
GIVEN `the requirement` IS A `Requirement made of me` @desc What the Commission required of you. One of: "a direction to produce documents or provide information or give a consent", "a summons to attend a conference or hearing", "a requirement to give evidence on oath or answer a question in the Workers Compensation Division", "a requirement to give evidence on oath or answer a question in the Police Officer Support Scheme Division"
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The most I can be fined in penalty units` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the requirement`
    WHEN `a direction to produce documents or provide information or give a consent` THEN 50
    WHEN `a summons to attend a conference or hearing` THEN 50
    WHEN `a requirement to give evidence on oath or answer a question in the Workers Compensation Division` THEN 50
    WHEN `a requirement to give evidence on oath or answer a question in the Police Officer Support Scheme Division` THEN 50

§§ `Sch 3 cl 7, Sch 4 cl 7 -- statements at the Commission and later court proceedings`

-- Sch 3 cl 7 "In proceedings before a court with respect to a claim for work injury damages (other than proceedings under
-- section 235A or 235C of the Workplace Injury Management and Workers Compensation Act 1998 or under the Crimes Act 1900
-- with respect to fraud), evidence of a statement made in proceedings before the Commission is not admissible unless the
-- person who made the statement agrees to the evidence being admitted."
-- Sch 4 cl 7(1) "In proceedings before a court with respect to a claim, evidence of a statement made during an assessment
-- conference is not admissible unless the person who made the statement agrees ..." (2) it "does not apply" to
-- proceedings under Division 6.6 of the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017 or Part 4.6 of the Motor Accidents Compensation
-- Act 1999. (3) "assessment conference means any conference or other proceeding held with or before the Commission in
-- connection with an assessment of a claim"; "claim" is a claim under either motor accidents Act.
-- With the maker's agreement, or in an excepted proceeding, the bar is lifted and the general law of evidence decides
-- (NOTHING; fork F3).
@export Can evidence of a statement made at the Personal Injury Commission be admitted in later court proceedings (Sch 3 cl 7; Sch 4 cl 7)?
GIVEN `the court proceedings` IS A `The court proceedings` @desc The court proceedings in which the statement would be used. One of: "court proceedings on a claim for work injury damages", "court proceedings under section 235A or 235C of the 1998 Act or for fraud under the Crimes Act 1900 on such a claim", "court proceedings on a motor accident claim", "court proceedings under Division 6.6 of the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017 or Part 4.6 of the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999", "other court proceedings"
      `the statement was made in proceedings before the Commission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement was made in proceedings before the Commission
      `the statement was made in an assessment conference on a motor accident claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement was made during a conference or other proceeding held with or before the Commission in connection with the assessment of a motor accident claim
      `the person who made it agrees` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who made the statement (you, or whoever else made it) agrees to the evidence being admitted
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can evidence of the statement be admitted in court` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the court proceedings`
    WHEN `court proceedings on a claim for work injury damages` THEN
        IF `the statement was made in proceedings before the Commission` AND (NOT `the person who made it agrees`)
        THEN JUST FALSE
        ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the bar does not apply; the general law of evidence decides)
    WHEN `court proceedings on a motor accident claim` THEN
        IF `the statement was made in an assessment conference on a motor accident claim` AND (NOT `the person who made it agrees`)
        THEN JUST FALSE
        ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the bar does not apply; the general law of evidence decides)
    OTHERWISE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (an excepted or other proceeding; the clauses do not apply)

§§ `ss 38, 40 -- assessors, reviewers and mediators as witnesses`

-- s 38 "A decision-maker [a medical assessor or a merit reviewer, s 32] is, in any legal proceedings, competent but not
-- compellable to give evidence or produce documents in respect of any matter in which the decision-maker was involved in
-- the course of the exercise of functions as a decision-maker." s 40 says the same of a mediator.
@export Can I compel the medical assessor, merit reviewer or mediator in my matter to give evidence or produce documents in legal proceedings (ss 38, 40)?
GIVEN `the officer` IS A `Commission officer` @desc Who you want to call. One of: "a registrar", "a medical assessor", "a merit reviewer", "a mediator", "a member of the Commission", "someone else working for the Commission"
      `it is about a matter they dealt with in that role` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence or documents concern a matter they were involved in while acting as a medical assessor, merit reviewer or mediator
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I compel them to give evidence` MEANS
    IF `the officer is a decision-maker or mediator` AND `it is about a matter they dealt with in that role`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (ss 38 and 40 do not reach this person or this matter)
    WHERE
        `the officer is a decision-maker or mediator` MEANS
               `the officer` EQUALS `a medical assessor`
            OR `the officer` EQUALS `a merit reviewer`
            OR `the officer` EQUALS `a mediator`

§§ `ss 52-54 -- the conduct of proceedings`

-- s 52(3) "If the Commission is satisfied that sufficient information has been supplied to it in connection with
-- proceedings, the Commission may exercise functions under this Act and enabling legislation without holding any
-- conference or formal hearing."
@export Can the Commission decide my matter without holding any conference or formal hearing (s 52(3))?
GIVEN `the Commission is satisfied it has sufficient information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission is satisfied that sufficient information has been supplied to it (its judgment; give its view)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my matter be decided without a conference or hearing` MEANS
    IF `the Commission is satisfied it has sufficient information`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 53(1) the President may replace a member after consideration has commenced and before determination "if ... the
-- member (a) becomes unavailable for any reason, or (b) ceases to be a member, or (c) ceases to have a qualification
-- required ...". (2) "The President may not replace a member unless the President has first (a) afforded the parties an
-- opportunity to make submissions about the proposed replacement, and (b) taken their submissions into account."
@export Can the member hearing my matter be replaced part-way through (s 53)?
GIVEN `the reason` IS A `Reason for replacing the member` @desc Why the member would be replaced. One of: "the member has become unavailable", "the member has ceased to be a member", "the member has ceased to have a qualification the proceedings require", "some other reason"
      `I was given an opportunity to make submissions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President gave the parties (including you) an opportunity to make submissions about the proposed replacement
      `the submissions were taken into account` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President took the parties' submissions into account
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the member hearing my matter be replaced` MEANS
    IF     (NOT (`the reason` EQUALS `some other reason`))
       AND `I was given an opportunity to make submissions`
       AND `the submissions were taken into account`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 54 "The Commission may at any stage dismiss proceedings before it (a) if it is satisfied that the proceedings have
-- been abandoned, or (b) if it is satisfied that the proceedings are frivolous or vexatious or otherwise misconceived or
-- lacking in substance, or (c) for any other ground of dismissal specified in the Commission rules." A JUST FALSE means
-- this section gives no ground on these facts.
@export Can the Commission dismiss my proceedings (s 54)?
GIVEN `the Commission is satisfied they are abandoned` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission is satisfied that the proceedings have been abandoned
      `the Commission is satisfied they lack substance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission is satisfied that the proceedings are frivolous or vexatious or otherwise misconceived or lacking in substance
      `a ground in the Commission rules applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a ground of dismissal specified in the Commission rules applies
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my proceedings be dismissed` MEANS
    IF `the Commission is satisfied they are abandoned` OR `the Commission is satisfied they lack substance` OR `a ground in the Commission rules applies`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `ss 56, 57 -- the decision`

-- s 56(1) "Except as otherwise provided by this Act or enabling legislation, a decision of the Commission under the
-- Workers Compensation Acts or in relation to the police officer support scheme is final and binding on the parties and
-- is not subject to appeal or review." Motor accidents decisions are outside s 56(1).
@export Is the Commission's decision in my matter final and binding, with no appeal or review (s 56(1))?
GIVEN `the Division` IS A `Commission Division` @desc The Division of the Commission that decided your matter. One of: "the Workers Compensation Division", "the Motor Accidents Division", "the Police Officer Support Scheme Division"
      `an appeal or review of it is provided for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this Act or the legislation under which the matter arose provides an appeal or review of this kind of decision (for example an appeal to a presidential member or to a medical Appeal Panel under the Workplace Injury Management and Workers Compensation Act 1998)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the decision final and binding` MEANS
    IF `the Division` EQUALS `the Motor Accidents Division`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 56(1) does not reach motor accidents decisions; the motor accidents legislation decides)
    ELSE IF `an appeal or review of it is provided for`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 56(2) "A decision of or proceeding before the Commission is not ... (b) liable to be challenged, appealed against,
-- reviewed, quashed or called into question by any court." (3) "this section does not limit any power of the Supreme
-- Court to provide relief for jurisdictional error in proceedings for the judicial review of a decision". Part 5 is
-- subject to enabling legislation (s 41(1)), so a court appeal that legislation provides stands (fork F4).
@export Can a court hear a challenge to the Commission's decision in my matter (s 56(2), (3))?
GIVEN `the challenge is for jurisdictional error` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you seek judicial review in the Supreme Court on the ground that the Commission made a jurisdictional error (an error going to its power to decide)
      `legislation gives a court appeal from it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legislation under which the matter arose gives an appeal or review of this decision to a court
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can a court hear a challenge to the decision` MEANS
    IF `the challenge is for jurisdictional error` OR `legislation gives a court appeal from it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 57(1) "The Commission may reconsider any matter that has been dealt with by the Commission in the Workers
-- Compensation Division or Police Officer Support Scheme Division and rescind, alter or amend any decision".
@export Can the Commission reconsider my matter and change its decision (s 57(1))?
GIVEN `the Division` IS A `Commission Division` @desc The Division of the Commission that dealt with your matter. One of: "the Workers Compensation Division", "the Motor Accidents Division", "the Police Officer Support Scheme Division"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Commission reconsider my matter` MEANS
    IF `the Division` EQUALS `the Motor Accidents Division`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 57(1) is limited in its own words to two Divisions; the text is silent on the third)
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 57(2) "If ... the President is satisfied that the decision contains an obvious error, the President may (a) alter the
-- decision to correct the error, or (b) direct a registrar to ..."; (3) a replacement certificate; (5) the replacement
-- prevails. (6) examples: clerical or typographical error, accidental slip or omission, defect of form, inconsistency
-- between the stated decision and the stated reasons.
@export Can an obvious error in the decision or certificate in my matter be corrected (s 57(2)-(5))?
GIVEN `the President is satisfied it is an obvious error` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President is satisfied that the decision contains an obvious error (the President's judgment; give its view)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the obvious error be corrected` MEANS
    IF `the President is satisfied it is an obvious error`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

@export Is this kind of error one of the examples of an obvious error the Act gives (s 57(6))?
GIVEN `the error` IS A `Kind of error` @desc The kind of error in the decision. One of: "a clerical or typographical error", "an accidental slip or omission", "a defect of form", "an inconsistency between the stated decision and the stated reasons", "some other error"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is this an example of an obvious error` MEANS
    IF `the error` EQUALS `some other error`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the examples are not exhaustive)
    ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `s 58 -- publication`

-- s 58(1) "The Commission must cause details of the following to be published in accordance with the Commission rules
-- (a) decisions of the Commission, (b) decisions of merit reviewers under Division 7.4 of the Motor Accident Injuries Act
-- 2017, (c) decisions of review panels for merit reviewers under [that] Division 7.4, (d) decisions of Appeal Panels for
-- medical assessments under Part 7 of Chapter 7 of the Workplace Injury Management and Workers Compensation Act 1998,
-- (e) decisions of review panels for medical assessments under Part 3.4 of the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or
-- Division 7.5 of the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017, (f) any other decisions prescribed by the Commission rules."
@export Will details of the decision in my matter be published (s 58(1))?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `Kind of decision` @desc Who made the decision. One of: "a decision of the Commission", "a decision of a merit reviewer under Division 7.4 of the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017", "a decision of a review panel for merit reviewers under that Division 7.4", "a decision of a medical Appeal Panel under Part 7 of Chapter 7 of the 1998 Act", "a decision of a medical review panel under Part 3.4 of the 1999 Act or Division 7.5 of the 2017 Act", "some other decision"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Will details of the decision be published` MEANS
    IF `the decision` EQUALS `some other decision`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the Commission rules, not deposited, may prescribe other decisions)
    ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `s 59 -- recovering an amount the Commission ordered`

-- s 59(1) "For the purposes of the recovery of any amount ordered to be paid by the Commission (including costs, but not
-- including a civil or other penalty), the amount is to be certified by the principal registrar." (2) the certificate
-- "must identify the person liable to pay". (3) "A certificate ... that is filed in the registry of a court having
-- jurisdiction to give judgment for a debt of the same amount ... operates as such a judgment." The court's monetary
-- limit is borrowed law, asked (fork F5 on identification).
@export Does the certificate for an amount the Commission ordered to be paid to me operate as a court judgment I can enforce (s 59)?
GIVEN `the amount is a penalty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the amount is a civil or other penalty (rather than compensation, costs or another amount ordered)
      `the principal registrar certified it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the principal registrar has certified the amount
      `the certificate names the person liable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the certificate identifies the person liable to pay
      `the certificate is filed in a court registry` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the certificate has been filed in the registry of a court
      `that court can give judgment for a debt of that amount` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that court has jurisdiction to give judgment for a debt of the amount in the certificate (for example, the Local Court's limit)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the certificate operate as a judgment` MEANS
    IF `the amount is a penalty`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 59 excludes penalties; it is silent on how they are recovered)
    ELSE IF     `the principal registrar certified it`
            AND `the certificate names the person liable`
            AND `the certificate is filed in a court registry`
            AND `that court can give judgment for a debt of that amount`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `ss 25-28 -- the District Court instead of the Commission (federal jurisdiction)`

-- s 26(1) "A person with standing to apply to the President or the Commission for a matter concerning a compensation claim
-- to be determined by the usual decision-maker (a compensation matter application) may, with the leave of the District
-- Court, make the application to the Court instead". (2) standing is for the regulations (not deposited; asked).
-- (3) "The District Court may grant leave ... only if it is satisfied that (a) an application was first made to the
-- President or Commission, and (b) the determination of the matter by the usual decision-maker would involve an exercise
-- of federal jurisdiction, and (c) the usual decision-maker would otherwise have had jurisdiction". "Federal
-- jurisdiction" is jurisdiction of a kind in s 75 or 76 of the Commonwealth Constitution (s 25), for example where the
-- parties are residents of different States.
@export Can the District Court give me leave to have my compensation matter decided there instead of by the Commission (ss 25, 26(1)-(3))?
GIVEN `my claim` IS A `My claim` @desc The claim the matter concerns. One of: "a claim for damages under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999", "a claim for statutory benefits under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017", "a claim for damages under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017", "a claim under the police officer support scheme", "a claim for workers compensation or work injury damages under the 1998 Act", "some other claim"
      `I have standing to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have standing to apply to the President or the Commission for the matter to be determined (as the regulations provide)
      `I first applied to the President or the Commission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your application was first made to the President or the Commission
      `deciding it would involve federal jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the District Court is satisfied that deciding the matter would involve an exercise of federal jurisdiction (for example, because the parties live in different States)
      `the usual decision-maker would otherwise have jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person or body that would normally decide the matter would otherwise have had jurisdiction to decide it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the District Court give me leave` MEANS
    IF     (NOT (`my claim` EQUALS `some other claim`))
       AND `I have standing to apply`
       AND `I first applied to the President or the Commission`
       AND `deciding it would involve federal jurisdiction`
       AND `the usual decision-maker would otherwise have jurisdiction`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 26(4) "An application for leave must be (a) filed with the District Court along with (i) an application that has
-- been completed in the form and manner required ... for the kind of compensation matter application concerned, and
-- (ii) if the parties ... have reached a settlement before leave is sought using a resolution process provided under this
-- Act or enabling legislation--a copy of the terms of settlement, and (b) accompanied by the applicable fee (if any)
-- payable for the compensation matter application unless it has already been paid."
@export What must I file with the District Court when I apply for leave (s 26(4))?
GIVEN `we settled using a resolution process before seeking leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties reached a settlement, before leave is sought, using a resolution process provided under this Act or the compensation legislation
      `the fee has already been paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the fee (if any) for the compensation matter application has already been paid
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`What must I file with my application for leave` MEANS
    `the application` FOLLOWED BY (`the completed application` FOLLOWED BY (`the settlement terms` `and then` `the fee`))
    WHERE
        `the application` MEANS "the application for leave, filed with the District Court"
        `the completed application` MEANS "the compensation matter application, completed in the form and manner required for that kind of application"
        `the settlement terms` MEANS
            IF `we settled using a resolution process before seeking leave`
            THEN LIST "a copy of the terms of settlement"
            ELSE EMPTY
        `the fee` MEANS
            IF `the fee has already been paid`
            THEN EMPTY
            ELSE LIST "the applicable fee (if any) for the compensation matter application"

GIVEN `the first list` IS A LIST OF STRING
      `the second list` IS A LIST OF STRING
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the first list` `and then` `the second list` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the first list`
    WHEN EMPTY THEN `the second list`
    WHEN x FOLLOWED BY xs THEN x FOLLOWED BY (xs `and then` `the second list`)

-- s 27(1) "If the District Court grants leave ... (a) proceedings for the determination of the application (substituted
-- proceedings) are taken to have been commenced in the Court on the day on which the application was first made".
@export If the District Court grants leave, on what day are my proceedings there taken to have started (s 27(1)(a))?
GIVEN `the Court granted leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the District Court has granted leave for the application to be made to it
      `the day I first made the application` IS A DATE @desc The day the application was first made (to the President or the Commission)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`On what day are my District Court proceedings taken to have started` MEANS
    IF `the Court granted leave`
    THEN JUST `the day I first made the application`
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (without leave, s 27 does not apply)

-- s 27(2) "Subsection (1) applies despite any limitation period under the Limitation Act 1969 or any enabling legislation
-- that applies to the application concerned provided it was first lodged for exercise by the usual decision-maker before
-- the expiry of the period." Lodging on the last day of the period is read as before its expiry (fork F6).
@export If the District Court grants leave, does s 27 keep my application in time despite the limitation period (s 27(1), (2))?
GIVEN `the Court granted leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the District Court has granted leave for the application to be made to it
      `the day I first lodged it with the usual decision-maker` IS A DATE @desc The day the application was first lodged with the Commission or other usual decision-maker
      `the last day of the limitation period` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the limitation period that applies to the application (under the Limitation Act 1969 or the compensation legislation)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does s 27 keep my application in time` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the Court granted leave`)
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (without leave, s 27 does not apply)
    ELSE IF `the day I first lodged it with the usual decision-maker` `is on or before` `the last day of the limitation period`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Sch 3 cl 6, Sch 3A cl 5 -- conciliation first`

-- Sch 3 cl 6(1) (and Sch 3A cl 5(1) in the same words) "The Commission constituted by a non-presidential member is not,
-- in the exercise of its substantive Division functions, to make an award or otherwise determine a dispute ... without
-- first using the member's best endeavours to bring the parties to the dispute to a settlement acceptable to all of
-- them." (2) no objection may be taken because the member tried to settle. Not asked for registrars' functions.
@export Must the member try to bring the parties to a settlement before deciding my dispute (Sch 3 cl 6; Sch 3A cl 5)?
GIVEN `the Division` IS A `Commission Division` @desc The Division of the Commission your dispute is in. One of: "the Workers Compensation Division", "the Motor Accidents Division", "the Police Officer Support Scheme Division"
      `the member is a non-presidential member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission is constituted by a non-presidential member (a principal, senior or general member, rather than the President or a Deputy President)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the member try to settle my dispute first` MEANS
    IF `the Division` EQUALS `the Motor Accidents Division`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the clauses are limited to two Divisions; the text is silent on the third)
    ELSE IF `the member is a non-presidential member`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the clauses are limited to non-presidential members; the text is silent on others)

§§ `Sch 5 -- complaints to the Independent Review Officer, and ILARS`

-- Sch 5 cl 1 "claimant means a person who makes or is entitled to make (a) a claim within the meaning of the Workplace
-- Injury Management and Workers Compensation Act 1998, or (b) a claim within the meaning of the Motor Accident Injuries
-- Act 2017, or (c) a claim within the meaning of the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999"; "insurer means a licensed
-- insurer under any of the enabling legislation". cl 8(1) "A claimant may complain to the Independent Review Officer
-- about any act or omission (including any decision or failure to decide) of an insurer that affects the entitlements,
-- rights or obligations of the claimant under the enabling legislation." A police officer support scheme claim is not in
-- the "means" list.
@export Can I complain to the Independent Review Officer about my insurer (Sch 5 cll 1, 8(1))?
GIVEN `my claim` IS A `My claim` @desc The claim you make or are entitled to make. One of: "a claim for damages under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999", "a claim for statutory benefits under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017", "a claim for damages under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017", "a claim under the police officer support scheme", "a claim for workers compensation or work injury damages under the 1998 Act", "some other claim"
      `the insurer is a licensed insurer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer you complain about is a licensed insurer under the workers compensation or motor accidents legislation
      `it affects my rights under that legislation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act, omission, decision or failure to decide you complain about affects your entitlements, rights or obligations under that legislation
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I complain to the Independent Review Officer` MEANS
    IF `my claim` EQUALS `a claim under the police officer support scheme` OR `my claim` EQUALS `some other claim`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF (NOT `the insurer is a licensed insurer`)
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `it affects my rights under that legislation`
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited in its own words; the text is silent on other complaints; fork F7)

-- cl 7(3) "The Independent Review Officer can decline to deal with a complaint if the claimant who makes the complaint
-- fails to comply with a request to provide information". cl 8(5) "may decline to deal with a complaint on the basis that
-- it is frivolous or vexatious or should not be dealt with for such other reason as the Independent Review Officer
-- considers relevant."
@export Can the Independent Review Officer decline to deal with my complaint (Sch 5 cll 7(3), 8(5))?
GIVEN `I failed to provide information the Officer asked for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed to comply with the Officer's request to provide information
      `the Officer considers it should not be dealt with` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Officer considers the complaint frivolous or vexatious, or that it should not be dealt with for another reason the Officer considers relevant
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Officer decline my complaint` MEANS
    IF `I failed to provide information the Officer asked for` OR `the Officer considers it should not be dealt with`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- cl 8(4) "The Independent Review Officer is to deal with a complaint within a period of 30 days after the complaint is
-- made unless the Independent Review Officer notifies the claimant and the insurer within that period that a specified
-- longer period will be required". The day the complaint is made is not counted, so the 30 days end on the 30th day after
-- it (fork F8). A notice after the 30 days does not extend the time; a notified period that ends sooner is not "longer"
-- and is ignored.
GIVEN `the day the complaint was made` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of the 30 days after` `the day the complaint was made` MEANS `the day the complaint was made` `plus days` 30

@export By what day must the Independent Review Officer deal with my complaint (Sch 5 cl 8(4))?
GIVEN `the day I made the complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the complaint
      `the Officer notified a longer period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Officer notified you and the insurer that a specified longer period will be required
      `the day the Officer notified it` IS A DATE @desc The day the Officer gave that notice (any day if none)
      `the last day of the longer period` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the longer period the Officer specified (any day if none)
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must my complaint be dealt with` MEANS
    IF     `the Officer notified a longer period`
       AND (`the day the Officer notified it` `is on or before` `the 30 days end`)
       AND (NOT (`the last day of the longer period` `is on or before` `the 30 days end`))
    THEN `the last day of the longer period`
    ELSE `the 30 days end`
    WHERE
        `the 30 days end` MEANS `the last day of the 30 days after` `the day I made the complaint`

-- The Officer's duty as a rule. Time 0 is the day the complaint is made, in days.
GIVEN `the days the Officer has` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Commission party` `Commission act`
`Sch 5 cl 8(4) -- the Independent Review Officer is to deal with the complaint in time` `the days the Officer has` MEANS
    PARTY `the Independent Review Officer`
    MUST `deal with the complaint`
    WITHIN `the days the Officer has`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the Independent Review Officer` BECAUSE "the complaint was not dealt with within 30 days after it was made, or the longer period notified within those 30 days (Sch 5 cl 8(4))"

-- cl 8(2) "The Independent Review Officer deals with a complaint by investigating the complaint and reporting to the
-- claimant and the insurer on the findings of the investigation, including the reasons for those findings."
@export Has the Independent Review Officer dealt with my complaint in time (Sch 5 cl 8(2), (4))?
GIVEN `the day I made the complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the complaint
      `the Officer notified a longer period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Officer notified you and the insurer that a specified longer period will be required
      `the day the Officer notified it` IS A DATE @desc The day the Officer gave that notice (any day if none)
      `the last day of the longer period` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the longer period the Officer specified (any day if none)
      `the Officer has reported to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Officer has reported to you and the insurer on the findings of the investigation, with reasons
      `the day the Officer reported` IS A DATE @desc The day the Officer reported (any day if not yet)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Where my complaint stands`
`Has my complaint been dealt with in time` MEANS
    IF `the Officer has reported to me`
    THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`Sch 5 cl 8(4) -- the Independent Review Officer is to deal with the complaint in time` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the Independent Review Officer` `deal with the complaint` (`the days from` `the day I made the complaint` `to` `the day the Officer reported`)))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the Officer dealt with my complaint in time`
         OTHERWISE `the Officer did not deal with my complaint in time`
    ELSE IF `today` `is on or before` `the deadline`
         THEN `the time for dealing with my complaint has not yet run out`
         ELSE `the Officer did not deal with my complaint in time`
    WHERE
        `the deadline` MEANS `By what day must my complaint be dealt with` `the day I made the complaint` `the Officer notified a longer period` `the day the Officer notified it` `the last day of the longer period`
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `the day I made the complaint` `to` `the deadline`

-- cl 9(2) "The purpose of ILARS is to provide funding for legal and associated costs for workers under the Workers
-- Compensation Acts seeking advice regarding decisions of insurers for those Acts and to provide assistance in finding
-- solutions for disputes between workers and insurers." Funding itself follows the ILARS guidelines (cl 10), not
-- deposited. cl 12 asks a parliamentary committee whether ILARS should extend to motor accident statutory benefits.
@export Is my matter within the purpose of ILARS, the Independent Legal Assistance and Review Service (Sch 5 cl 9(2))?
GIVEN `I am a worker under the Workers Compensation Acts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker under the workers compensation legislation (rather than, for example, a person injured in a motor accident)
      `I seek advice on an insurer's decision or help with a dispute with an insurer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are seeking legal advice about a decision of a workers compensation insurer, or help in finding a solution to a dispute with one
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my matter within the purpose of ILARS` MEANS
    IF `I am a worker under the Workers Compensation Acts` AND `I seek advice on an insurer's decision or help with a dispute with an insurer`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a purpose clause, not closed with "only"; the text is silent on other matters)

§§ `s 64 -- protection of Commission officers`

-- s 5(1) "relevant Commission officer" means a registrar, a medical assessor, a merit reviewer, a mediator, or another
-- kind of person prescribed by the regulations (not deposited; not asked). s 64(3) "Any thing done or omitted to be done
-- by a relevant Commission officer in exercising the functions ... does not, if the thing was done or omitted to be done
-- in good faith, subject the relevant Commission officer personally to any action, liability, claim or demand." (4) "Any
-- liability that would attach to a person were it not for the operation of subsection (3) attaches instead to the
-- Crown." Members of the Commission are protected by Sch 2 cl 4 (out of scope).
@export Can I hold this Commission officer personally liable for something they did or failed to do in my matter (s 64(3))?
GIVEN `the officer` IS A `Commission officer` @desc Who the officer is. One of: "a registrar", "a medical assessor", "a merit reviewer", "a mediator", "a member of the Commission", "someone else working for the Commission"
      `they were exercising their functions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did it (or failed to do it) in exercising the functions given to them by or under this or any other Act
      `they acted in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they acted in good faith (decided by a court; give its view)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I hold the officer personally liable` MEANS
    IF `s 64(3) protects the officer` `the officer` `they were exercising their functions` `they acted in good faith`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 64(3) does not reach this person or act; the general law decides)

@export Does the liability fall on the Crown instead of the Commission officer (s 64(4))?
GIVEN `the officer` IS A `Commission officer` @desc Who the officer is. One of: "a registrar", "a medical assessor", "a merit reviewer", "a mediator", "a member of the Commission", "someone else working for the Commission"
      `they were exercising their functions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did it (or failed to do it) in exercising the functions given to them by or under this or any other Act
      `they acted in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they acted in good faith (decided by a court; give its view)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the liability fall on the Crown` MEANS
    IF `s 64(3) protects the officer` `the officer` `they were exercising their functions` `they acted in good faith`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 64(4) does not apply; the general law decides)

GIVEN `the officer` IS A `Commission officer`, `exercising functions` IS A BOOLEAN, `good faith` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 64(3) protects the officer` `the officer` `exercising functions` `good faith` IF
        (    `the officer` EQUALS `a registrar`
         OR  `the officer` EQUALS `a medical assessor`
         OR  `the officer` EQUALS `a merit reviewer`
         OR  `the officer` EQUALS `a mediator`)
    AND `exercising functions`
    AND `good faith`
